WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:06.290
After getting soaked the day before – another
night of heavy rain finally sealed the fate

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.440
of the Benito Juarez shelter in downtown Tijuana.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.690
The 6000-plus migrants from Middle America
housed in the former sports complex did not

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.450
stand a chance against the downpour.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.290
Only the children still manage a gentle smile.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:28.310
The adults are mourning their tents floating
in ankle deep muddy water, their clothes and

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.050
other belongings drenched, ruined.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.250
For many, a total loss.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.610
And amid appalling sanitary conditions -Tijuana
city and Mexican federal officials saw no

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:45.109
other option but to shut down the shelter…

00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:49.380
Buses arrive and open their doors – they
promise to bring these people to a “better,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.130
safer place”
Volunteers are not hard to find.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:01:01.899
Within hours nearly a thousand migrants are
willing to make yet another journey.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:07.080
Taking with them the little they deemed worth
saving, they arrive here: El Barretal.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.240
A former bus parking facility and concert
venue.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.140
About 20 km from downtown Tijuana and - far
away from the border.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:23.630
For some Tijuana migrants this new shelter
seems to bring some improvements:

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.650
Here the ground is concrete, not mud.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:27.979
That’s better.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:34.320
But most important is to have a roof over
our heads that protects us from the cold.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.729
Others see the benefits of the new shelter
but are wary of its location.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:44.830
The real reason for the police to bring us
here is that they don’t want us close to

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.830
the border.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:50.230
Imagine, now we need to spend 100 pesos to
get to the border for asylum-paperwork.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:55.490
We don’t have the money and it’s going
to take us all day to get there.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.210
Now it’s even more difficult for us to get
into the United States.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.009
Our dream is to be there.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:07.810
Meanwhile back at the shelter in downtown
Tijuana the line for the buses is even longer

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:08.810
now.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.110
More migrants seem to be willing to leave
for the new shelter - sooner rather than later.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:21.890
A look inside the old place may reveal the
reason

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:29.209
“When I was a little girl”, she sings,
“I knew only the beauty of Honduras.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:35.180
But as time passed by and I grew older I understood,
I should not love this land.”

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.400
The sad tune strikes a nerve with many on
this day, as they continue to harbor their

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.590
dreams of a future in a better place

